00:47:35.720 --> 00:47:40.510
but I feel so much that you
feel, and I related so much,

00:47:40.510 --> 00:47:43.213
that this is my struggle
right now is walking

00:47:43.213 --> 00:47:47.120
in spirit and flesh at the same time.

00:47:47.120 --> 00:47:48.713
And it's such a struggle.

00:47:49.845 --> 00:47:52.010
I just talked to people today about that.

00:47:52.010 --> 00:47:56.633
And I was wondering if you
had what you do to do that.

00:47:58.010 --> 00:48:00.990
It seems the closer I get
to God, the more the dark

00:48:00.990 --> 00:48:02.203
side comes up.

00:48:05.120 --> 00:48:07.243
I think it's, that's one question anyway.

00:48:09.550 --> 00:48:12.490
And do you believe like
in spiritual deliverance

00:48:12.490 --> 00:48:15.523
and things like that, like
if you'd gotten any prayer,

00:48:16.430 --> 00:48:18.810
'cause I know that there's
a lot of spiritual bondage

00:48:18.810 --> 00:48:21.870
when you get into the abuse and the way

00:48:21.870 --> 00:48:24.550
that you've lived in
abandonment, you mentioned...

00:48:26.630 --> 00:48:28.053
What was the word you used?

00:48:33.050 --> 00:48:36.890
When you were, the trauma
from when your dad left,

00:48:36.890 --> 00:48:39.040
there was abandonment
and something else...

00:48:40.720 --> 00:48:43.930
Anyway, I'm just
wondering what you thought

00:48:43.930 --> 00:48:46.160
about that because I'm
very involved in church

00:48:46.160 --> 00:48:49.105
and I do understand that there's
a lot of spiritual bondage

00:48:49.105 --> 00:48:52.667
and wondering what you thought about that.

00:48:54.592 --> 00:48:55.425
- You.

00:48:55.425 --> 00:48:56.543
- Great question.

00:48:58.960 --> 00:49:03.120
So yes, part of it,

00:49:03.120 --> 00:49:05.383
for me, was understanding,

00:49:08.080 --> 00:49:10.270
healing is a process, right?

00:49:10.270 --> 00:49:14.150
So it's a continual process.

00:49:14.150 --> 00:49:19.150
And I tried to think of a time
in my life where God just,

00:49:20.040 --> 00:49:22.550
he didn't come through and I
couldn't think of one, right?

00:49:22.550 --> 00:49:25.900
So that was part of the process
was saying he's dependable.

00:49:25.900 --> 00:49:29.910
So let me trust him in this
particular circumstance.

00:49:29.910 --> 00:49:34.910
And then there's so many
different examples, like,

00:49:35.930 --> 00:49:39.430
as I looked at Job and looked at his life

00:49:39.430 --> 00:49:42.010
and as I looked at
Lamentations and just looked

00:49:42.010 --> 00:49:45.960
at Moses and it's like,
he's not gonna, Joshua,

00:49:45.960 --> 00:49:47.440
he's not gonna take you all the way

00:49:47.440 --> 00:49:49.654
through the wilderness to
the edge of the promised

00:49:49.654 --> 00:49:52.350
land and then say, "Bye!"

00:49:52.350 --> 00:49:53.183
Right?

00:49:53.183 --> 00:49:57.250
So it's like, there's a
process of coming through all

00:49:57.250 --> 00:49:59.360
of that in order to get to this place.

00:49:59.360 --> 00:50:01.350
And that process involves trust.

00:50:01.350 --> 00:50:05.130
And for me, it was him
inviting me to trust him

00:50:05.130 --> 00:50:07.014
in the midst of that madness.

00:50:07.014 --> 00:50:12.014
I think personally, it
was very helpful for me

00:50:13.700 --> 00:50:15.650
because I tend to wear all of the burden

00:50:15.650 --> 00:50:16.620
on my own shoulders.

00:50:16.620 --> 00:50:19.580
It was helpful for me
to know that God wants

00:50:19.580 --> 00:50:20.920
to carry those burdens.

00:50:20.920 --> 00:50:22.510
It was helpful for me
to know that he wants me

00:50:22.510 --> 00:50:27.210
dependent and broken and not
perfect and put together.

00:50:27.210 --> 00:50:28.960
And so I could relinquish a lot

00:50:28.960 --> 00:50:33.630
of this that I had weighing
on me in his hands.

00:50:33.630 --> 00:50:36.270
And I know that sounds a
ethereal, just out there,

00:50:36.270 --> 00:50:41.020
but part of it was for
me looking in the text,

00:50:41.020 --> 00:50:43.590
looking at scriptures and
seeing promises and seeing

00:50:43.590 --> 00:50:48.400
these realities flesh themselves
out and stop trying to be,

00:50:48.400 --> 00:50:50.870
or not be my earthly father and focus

00:50:50.870 --> 00:50:52.387
on my heavenly father, you know?

00:50:52.387 --> 00:50:54.453
And that was a big piece of it for me.

00:50:57.798 --> 00:50:59.762
- [Audience Member] Rejection. What else?

00:50:59.762 --> 00:51:01.410
Abandonment and rejection.

00:51:01.410 --> 00:51:02.430
- Rejection. Yeah.

00:51:02.430 --> 00:51:03.263
I'm accepted.

00:51:03.263 --> 00:51:05.460
So that was the biggest piece
too, is I'm accepted, right?

00:51:05.460 --> 00:51:08.290
Like if I, if I'm accepted by
the creator of all creation,

00:51:08.290 --> 00:51:11.520
why am I fighting for
everyone else's acceptance?

00:51:11.520 --> 00:51:14.013
Doesn't make sense, right?

00:51:15.128 --> 00:51:16.593
- Thank you.

00:51:17.580 --> 00:51:18.573
Someone else?

00:51:27.010 --> 00:51:28.083
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

00:51:29.730 --> 00:51:32.583
How to bring up a child
from the age of one week,

00:51:34.920 --> 00:51:38.833
show that the child
will be confident, kind,

00:51:42.090 --> 00:51:46.460
in contrast to being spoiled
by getting a gold star

00:51:46.460 --> 00:51:47.453
for doing nothing.

00:51:50.780 --> 00:51:52.120
- Participation trophies,

00:51:52.120 --> 00:51:54.314
I take it you're not a fan of those.

00:51:54.314 --> 00:51:57.147
(audience laughs)

00:52:00.410 --> 00:52:05.172
Yeah, I mean. Again, so let me say this.

00:52:05.172 --> 00:52:06.005
- And how are you doing this?

00:52:06.005 --> 00:52:06.838
- Yeah. So let me say this.

00:52:06.838 --> 00:52:08.560
So another thing I
talked about earlier was

00:52:08.560 --> 00:52:12.068
it's very important for
me to be healthy if I want

00:52:12.068 --> 00:52:16.560
to create health in my family.

00:52:16.560 --> 00:52:19.020
It's very important for me
to have a proper perspective

00:52:19.020 --> 00:52:19.853
of who I am and to have.

00:52:19.853 --> 00:52:24.853
A lot of the areas of my
mental health and trauma

00:52:25.270 --> 00:52:26.390
were identity issues.

00:52:26.390 --> 00:52:27.840
I didn't know who I was.

00:52:27.840 --> 00:52:30.400
I didn't know who I was created to be.

00:52:30.400 --> 00:52:34.420
And so success was always
me comparing myself

00:52:34.420 --> 00:52:38.160
to other people, instead
of comparing myself

00:52:38.160 --> 00:52:40.333
to what I was supposed to do,

00:52:41.410 --> 00:52:44.240
to what I had a vision to accomplish.

00:52:44.240 --> 00:52:46.530
It had really nothing to do
with everyone else around me.

00:52:46.530 --> 00:52:49.970
It should have had more to do
with the vision that I had.

00:52:49.970 --> 00:52:52.240
And so for me it's important to have,

00:52:52.240 --> 00:52:54.402
to take proper care of myself first,

00:52:54.402 --> 00:52:58.130
before I take care of my kids.

00:52:58.130 --> 00:53:02.000
Because if I'm not healthy,
I have nothing to offer them.

00:53:02.000 --> 00:53:06.410
And then you will tend
to, put it like this,

00:53:06.410 --> 00:53:09.160
if you have what I would
call like functional saviors

00:53:09.160 --> 00:53:13.340
or scapegoats to deal
with your issues in life,

00:53:13.340 --> 00:53:16.480
you're going to reimplement
that for your children.

00:53:16.480 --> 00:53:20.170
So if you think money will
solve all of your problems,

00:53:20.170 --> 00:53:22.700
then you're gonna teach your
children the same thing.

00:53:22.700 --> 00:53:24.670
And they're gonna think
that there's some kind

00:53:24.670 --> 00:53:26.144
of crutch can lean on.

00:53:26.144 --> 00:53:31.012
I stopped rewarding my
children with desserts

00:53:31.012 --> 00:53:34.677
and things along those
lines when they do well.

00:53:34.677 --> 00:53:37.890
And the reason why is
because I didn't want them

00:53:37.890 --> 00:53:42.100
to feel like eating
unhealthy was how they should

00:53:42.100 --> 00:53:44.190
reward themselves in life.

00:53:44.190 --> 00:53:45.023
Right?

00:53:45.023 --> 00:53:48.670
Because then that, and I, so
it's like, oh, you're sad?

00:53:48.670 --> 00:53:51.003
Come on, let's go get a cheeseburger.

00:53:51.003 --> 00:53:53.580
I mean, cheer you up
with an ice cream cone,

00:53:53.580 --> 00:53:56.070
because then what does that
teach them down the road?

00:53:56.070 --> 00:53:57.530
Like, well, I'm sad,

00:53:57.530 --> 00:53:59.130
I'm gonna eat this whole chocolate cake,

00:53:59.130 --> 00:54:00.550
because that's what...
(audience laughs)

00:54:00.550 --> 00:54:04.350
Right? So, but I have to
know that about myself first.

00:54:04.350 --> 00:54:06.640
And so a lot of it has
been me having to learn

00:54:06.640 --> 00:54:08.570
these particular things.

00:54:08.570 --> 00:54:13.570
- Thank you. The mic runners
are in charge. (laughs)

00:54:15.550 --> 00:54:18.627
I see multiple hands. There's
one here in the front.

00:54:23.090 --> 00:54:24.810
- [Audience Member] Thank
you so much for your talk

00:54:24.810 --> 00:54:26.510
and your vulnerability.

00:54:26.510 --> 00:54:31.202
I'm wondering how you use your trauma

00:54:31.202 --> 00:54:36.202
and or what your experience
with mental health in your art,

00:54:37.300 --> 00:54:42.300
in, and I'm wondering if
you do use it, if you,

00:54:43.660 --> 00:54:46.840
'cause I'm wondering about
the idea of expressing

00:54:46.840 --> 00:54:49.480
yourself as a holistic person,

00:54:49.480 --> 00:54:51.770
just if you could talk about that.

00:54:51.770 --> 00:54:53.240
- Yeah. I think that's good.

00:54:53.240 --> 00:54:56.830
I think expression,
specifically like in art,

00:54:56.830 --> 00:54:59.950
is a part of owning your narrative, right?

00:54:59.950 --> 00:55:02.040
And when you can own your narrative,

00:55:02.040 --> 00:55:05.850
it's no longer a tool of
oppression against you.

00:55:05.850 --> 00:55:08.420
It's a tool of empowerment, right?

00:55:08.420 --> 00:55:11.120
So I can own my narrative and I own it.

00:55:11.120 --> 00:55:16.120
It's the, one of the most
amazing things Eminem ever did,

00:55:16.550 --> 00:55:21.550
way back when, was when
he wrote "Lose Yourself".

00:55:21.740 --> 00:55:23.080
And he did the whole "8 Mile" movie,

00:55:23.080 --> 00:55:24.250
he cut his own legs off.

00:55:24.250 --> 00:55:26.120
He acknowledged his own weaknesses

00:55:26.120 --> 00:55:28.210
before anyone else could.

00:55:28.210 --> 00:55:32.680
And for me, art has been a
place to acknowledge openly

00:55:32.680 --> 00:55:35.063
my weaknesses, acknowledge my struggles.

00:55:36.010 --> 00:55:39.520
Now, sometimes I'm
acknowledging them for myself,

00:55:39.520 --> 00:55:41.400
not for the world to hear,

00:55:41.400 --> 00:55:45.690
but specifically when
there's weaknesses or issues

00:55:45.690 --> 00:55:48.240
that I have processed and I have gone

00:55:48.240 --> 00:55:49.820
through and I've gotten help with,

00:55:49.820 --> 00:55:51.420
and I have been counseled through,

00:55:51.420 --> 00:55:54.270
I don't mind talking about those publicly

00:55:54.270 --> 00:55:56.070
because I'm on the other side of it.

00:55:56.070 --> 00:55:57.743
And they're not,

00:55:59.310 --> 00:56:02.350
they're not stories for me to be

00:56:02.350 --> 00:56:06.000
ashamed of, they're stories
that I triumphed through,

00:56:06.000 --> 00:56:07.683
and have made me a
better version of myself.

00:56:07.683 --> 00:56:09.220
And so art is,

00:56:09.220 --> 00:56:11.690
it has been very therapeutic
for me in that regard

00:56:11.690 --> 00:56:13.900
because I can talk about
it and I can express it.

00:56:13.900 --> 00:56:17.960
I don't think every piece of
art needs to be heard or seen,

00:56:17.960 --> 00:56:20.840
but they need to be created for sure.

00:56:20.840 --> 00:56:22.090
Yeah.

00:56:22.090 --> 00:56:23.110
- There's a student here.

00:56:23.110 --> 00:56:25.447
- [Audience Member] Oh,
okay. So I have a question.

00:56:25.447 --> 00:56:29.240
How would you necessarily
respond to someone?

00:56:29.240 --> 00:56:31.560
Say like in the church who
says like mental illness

00:56:31.560 --> 00:56:33.870
doesn't exist, just pray about it,

00:56:33.870 --> 00:56:36.590
God's gonna handle it and
just leave it at that.

00:56:36.590 --> 00:56:38.530
And then my second question is how do

00:56:38.530 --> 00:56:40.474
you view like suicide?

00:56:40.474 --> 00:56:43.250
And in church, I can't
remember the pastor's name,

00:56:43.250 --> 00:56:44.710
but more recently in the media,

00:56:44.710 --> 00:56:49.310
there was a pastor who died
by suicide and it's been

00:56:49.310 --> 00:56:51.910
like more so on the
forefront with the church.

00:56:51.910 --> 00:56:54.530
And people saying like, by him doing that,

00:56:54.530 --> 00:56:57.440
he was selfish and condemning him.

00:56:57.440 --> 00:56:59.120
And like, God doesn't like that.

00:56:59.120 --> 00:57:01.080
Like how would you respond to like

00:57:01.080 --> 00:57:03.220
the criticisms like that?

00:57:03.220 --> 00:57:04.380
- Yeah.

00:57:04.380 --> 00:57:06.603
I'm very sensitive,

00:57:08.280 --> 00:57:10.450
specifically when I think we say

00:57:10.450 --> 00:57:14.050
the church and I think
there's this homogenistic

00:57:14.050 --> 00:57:17.070
kind of perspective of what that means.

00:57:17.070 --> 00:57:19.270
And so it's easy to, like, say,

00:57:19.270 --> 00:57:20.670
this is what The Church thinks.

00:57:20.670 --> 00:57:23.417
The church is not a homogeny,

00:57:23.417 --> 00:57:27.930
there's individuals
within that have unique

00:57:27.930 --> 00:57:29.270
perspectives on things.

00:57:29.270 --> 00:57:33.210
And some of them you vehemently
disagree with and some

00:57:33.210 --> 00:57:34.860
you love and you do agree with.

00:57:34.860 --> 00:57:39.860
And so as it pertains to
like what the church says

00:57:40.050 --> 00:57:42.631
about mental health, I don't know.

00:57:42.631 --> 00:57:47.470
I know that the church
that I interact with sees

00:57:47.470 --> 00:57:52.470
it as necessary, sees it
as very beneficial to seek

00:57:54.030 --> 00:57:58.540
self care and counsel and
therapy and whatever's necessary.

00:57:58.540 --> 00:58:02.593
And so I can speak on behalf
of those group of people.

00:58:04.740 --> 00:58:06.550
Those who would say that, I would say,

00:58:06.550 --> 00:58:09.860
tell them to stop going
to bed every night then.

00:58:09.860 --> 00:58:12.760
'Cause if you just need to pray
about it, then don't sleep.

00:58:12.760 --> 00:58:14.760
Just pray about it. You
don't need to sleep.

00:58:14.760 --> 00:58:16.710
Why are you sleeping? Just pray about it.

00:58:16.710 --> 00:58:18.230
God will deliver you and you can make it,

00:58:18.230 --> 00:58:19.063
you know what I mean?

00:58:19.063 --> 00:58:20.870
It's like, it's just...

00:58:20.870 --> 00:58:25.020
So we're here as

00:58:27.200 --> 00:58:30.690
crooked sticks drawing
straight lines, right?

00:58:30.690 --> 00:58:35.690
And so we're not here to
be perfect androids, right?

00:58:38.090 --> 00:58:42.070
We're here as examples of
what a great God can do

00:58:42.070 --> 00:58:44.470
with broken people.

00:58:44.470 --> 00:58:46.500
And I think that's the difference there.

00:58:46.500 --> 00:58:48.390
And so, and that,

00:58:48.390 --> 00:58:52.720
I think that carries over
into criminal activity,

00:58:52.720 --> 00:58:56.710
that carries over into depression,

00:58:56.710 --> 00:58:59.160
that carries over into
all of those particular

00:58:59.160 --> 00:59:01.076
issues, suicide included.

00:59:01.076 --> 00:59:06.076
That that's evidence of
broken, hurting people, right?

00:59:06.120 --> 00:59:07.827
It's not that...

00:59:07.827 --> 00:59:09.210
There's no reason to cast judgment,

00:59:09.210 --> 00:59:11.850
to throw stones in that
particular circumstance.

00:59:11.850 --> 00:59:15.190
That's just the evidence
of broken people that God

00:59:15.190 --> 00:59:18.320
still uses in different
ways that are beyond

00:59:18.320 --> 00:59:19.740
our comprehension at times.

00:59:19.740 --> 00:59:21.063
So, my perspective.

00:59:29.190 --> 00:59:31.760
- So additional questions
that some of you submitted

00:59:31.760 --> 00:59:35.897
ahead of time, just to know
that there're more questions.

00:59:35.897 --> 00:59:38.680
What are some of the
barriers that prevent people

00:59:38.680 --> 00:59:41.560
from seeking out mental health services?

00:59:41.560 --> 00:59:44.410
What is the current state of mental health

00:59:44.410 --> 00:59:48.683
conversations in our society,
on college campuses at Yale?

00:59:50.270 --> 00:59:55.270
How do you kind of have an
effective work-life balance?

00:59:56.600 --> 01:00:00.270
I wanna give us another
opportunity to interact

01:00:00.270 --> 01:00:02.513
with your neighbor,
the person next to you.

01:00:03.360 --> 01:00:06.260
First, take a minute
and think in the realm

01:00:06.260 --> 01:00:09.880
of social justice, what
are some of the positive

01:00:09.880 --> 01:00:14.429
changes that you've tried
to make in your life

01:00:14.429 --> 01:00:18.180
or the life of those
around you in the midst

01:00:18.180 --> 01:00:22.623
of ongoing social justice challenges?

01:00:23.890 --> 01:00:26.950
So what are some of the positive changes

01:00:26.950 --> 01:00:30.900
that you've tried to make
in your life or the lives

01:00:30.900 --> 01:00:34.145
of those around you in
the midst of ongoing

01:00:34.145 --> 01:00:37.990
social justice challenges?

01:00:37.990 --> 01:00:41.116
So give me about 10 minutes just to think

01:00:41.116 --> 01:00:44.063
about it or 10 seconds to think about it.

01:00:44.063 --> 01:00:45.743
10 minutes is a long time, right?

01:00:45.743 --> 01:00:47.198
- Yale people, think!

01:00:47.198 --> 01:00:50.031
(audience laughs)

01:00:51.300 --> 01:00:52.510
- And when you're ready, you know,

01:00:52.510 --> 01:00:54.710
share it with the person next to you,

01:00:54.710 --> 01:00:56.720
have a conversation with
the person next to you,

01:00:56.720 --> 01:00:59.140
and then we'll come back to additional

01:00:59.140 --> 01:01:00.863
questions and comments.

01:01:05.970 --> 01:01:08.030
This is good. This is good.

01:01:08.030 --> 01:01:09.603
Excellent. This is good.

01:01:11.050 --> 01:01:12.024
Hopefully you weren't talking

01:01:12.024 --> 01:01:12.857
about what's in the dining hall,

01:01:12.857 --> 01:01:15.491
but you were actually in
conversation about what,

01:01:15.491 --> 01:01:17.640
what's the importance to you.

01:01:17.640 --> 01:01:19.590
If you would go ahead and share?

01:01:19.590 --> 01:01:24.590
- Yeah. So social justice...

01:01:26.064 --> 01:01:27.510
And there's many facets of it, right?

01:01:27.510 --> 01:01:31.040
And I've been involved in a
number of different facets

01:01:31.040 --> 01:01:35.003
of what we would say social justice is.

01:01:36.030 --> 01:01:38.620
It's made me a better
person in a multitude

01:01:38.620 --> 01:01:39.453
of different ways.

01:01:39.453 --> 01:01:43.620
So on one, point one,

01:01:43.620 --> 01:01:46.383
it's made me stop seeing
myself as a commodity,

01:01:47.470 --> 01:01:48.560
being able to be bought,

01:01:48.560 --> 01:01:53.253
because there's a sacrifice
that I had to make financially,

01:01:54.520 --> 01:01:56.630
commercially, whatever you would call it,

01:01:56.630 --> 01:02:01.630
to say things that weren't
palatable for parts of society.

01:02:02.300 --> 01:02:05.190
And it's easy to just kind
of try to toe the line

01:02:05.190 --> 01:02:08.790
and walk the fence in order to secure.

01:02:08.790 --> 01:02:10.040
It showed, I mean,

01:02:10.040 --> 01:02:11.940
it exposed parts of my heart that were

01:02:12.842 --> 01:02:15.270
just financially motivated.

01:02:15.270 --> 01:02:17.600
And I'm not saying we
shouldn't be responsible,

01:02:17.600 --> 01:02:19.710
but I'm saying we should care.

01:02:19.710 --> 01:02:24.710
And so I would go from doing
a show for 3,000 people.

01:02:26.590 --> 01:02:29.810
And then as I started to
raise my voice on issues

01:02:29.810 --> 01:02:34.810
of police brutality, those
shows shrink dramatically.

01:02:35.020 --> 01:02:40.020
And so did my account. But it freed me.

01:02:40.780 --> 01:02:43.340
It freed me to be myself.

01:02:43.340 --> 01:02:48.340
Another way that it made me
better was, it made me very...

01:02:48.950 --> 01:02:51.500
Because of the issues that I
saw happening in my community,

01:02:51.500 --> 01:02:53.120
specifically the black community,

01:02:53.120 --> 01:02:55.750
it made me more attuned
to other communities.

01:02:55.750 --> 01:02:59.240
And so then I had to ask
myself, how misogynistic,

01:02:59.240 --> 01:03:01.100
how patriarchal am I?

01:03:01.100 --> 01:03:03.800
And how can I care about
these issues of black people,

01:03:03.800 --> 01:03:06.130
but not care about the issues of women?

01:03:06.130 --> 01:03:06.963
Right?

01:03:06.963 --> 01:03:09.844
And so I had to start
digging more and growing

01:03:09.844 --> 01:03:13.100
in multitude of different
other areas, other ethnicities,

01:03:13.100 --> 01:03:18.100
and begin to care about it
as a global issue as well.

01:03:18.670 --> 01:03:22.193
- Thank you. Are there four
of us who can contribute?

01:03:24.130 --> 01:03:27.433
So there is a hand here. Yeah.

01:03:29.930 --> 01:03:30.900
- [Audience Member] Hi.

01:03:30.900 --> 01:03:33.460
Speaking about community, I was just,

01:03:33.460 --> 01:03:35.860
I just wanted to know what is the role

01:03:37.010 --> 01:03:42.010
that a community can play
in the healing process

01:03:43.720 --> 01:03:45.810
and in addressing mental health issues,

01:03:45.810 --> 01:03:49.766
specifically to you, in your experience,

01:03:49.766 --> 01:03:53.870
did you have a community with
which to channel, to talk,

01:03:53.870 --> 01:03:58.533
to consult, to go through
what you're going through.

01:03:59.472 --> 01:04:02.483
And my second question is,

01:04:03.680 --> 01:04:08.680
how do we as Yale students
from where the communities,

01:04:08.770 --> 01:04:10.089
where we come from?

01:04:10.089 --> 01:04:12.210
Like, for example, I
come from South Africa.

01:04:12.210 --> 01:04:16.600
So how do I go back and
like create dialogue

01:04:16.600 --> 01:04:18.300
in the community where I've come from,

01:04:18.300 --> 01:04:21.950
and particularly in
marginalized communities.

01:04:21.950 --> 01:04:26.780
So where like people
feel that seemingly more

01:04:26.780 --> 01:04:31.780
pressing issues than mental
health, for example, poverty,

01:04:32.930 --> 01:04:36.363
unemployment, drugs, you
know, those are seemingly,

01:04:37.540 --> 01:04:41.743
at least people feel that
they are urgent and important

01:04:41.743 --> 01:04:43.440
than actually mental health.

01:04:43.440 --> 01:04:46.880
So how do we create a
work in such a community?

01:04:46.880 --> 01:04:49.940
How do you create conversations
and how do we bring

01:04:49.940 --> 01:04:53.230
about that intersection?

01:04:53.230 --> 01:04:55.107
So how do you bring the faith community

01:04:55.107 --> 01:04:57.072
to that conversation?

01:04:57.072 --> 01:04:59.450
How do you bring the
culture to that community

01:04:59.450 --> 01:05:02.290
and also social justice as well.

01:05:02.290 --> 01:05:03.550
So those are two questions.

01:05:03.550 --> 01:05:06.540
Basically I have your personal
journey with community

01:05:06.540 --> 01:05:10.041
and also how do we create
these conversations

01:05:10.041 --> 01:05:12.803
in the communities where we come from?

01:05:14.010 --> 01:05:14.843
- I should be brief,

01:05:14.843 --> 01:05:17.240
'cause I noticed a lot of
other comments as well.

01:05:17.240 --> 01:05:18.990
Where in South Africa are you from?

01:05:20.210 --> 01:05:21.953
- [Audience Member]
I'm from Port Elizabeth

01:05:21.953 --> 01:05:25.130
but came to as like my second home.

01:05:25.130 --> 01:05:27.750
- Right on, I just got
back from South Africa.

01:05:27.750 --> 01:05:30.080
So shout out to you. Congratulations.

01:05:30.080 --> 01:05:30.943
You made it.

01:05:33.760 --> 01:05:35.910
So, on question two,

01:05:35.910 --> 01:05:38.470
you talked about how to you
lead in those particular.

01:05:38.470 --> 01:05:40.750
I think leaders lead in
vulnerability, right?

01:05:40.750 --> 01:05:43.573
So you've got to be the
vulnerable one and show

01:05:43.573 --> 01:05:46.320
the example of how

01:05:49.460 --> 01:05:51.690
you're still here,

01:05:51.690 --> 01:05:56.380
irregardless of the
scrutiny or whatever you may

01:05:56.380 --> 01:06:01.380
have faced for being honest
about issues of mental health,

01:06:02.680 --> 01:06:06.178
issues of societal
injustice, social injustice,

01:06:06.178 --> 01:06:09.430
you addressed it, you lived through it,

01:06:09.430 --> 01:06:13.422
you still got all your
limbs and you're sitting

01:06:13.422 --> 01:06:15.790
at Yale University, right?

01:06:15.790 --> 01:06:19.950
So I think you are a living
breathing example for a lot

01:06:19.950 --> 01:06:22.190
of people, specifically in
those marginalized communities,

01:06:22.190 --> 01:06:23.620
that it can be done.

01:06:23.620 --> 01:06:24.890
You give them the freedom,

01:06:24.890 --> 01:06:28.590
you give them the power to do
that, to the first question,

01:06:28.590 --> 01:06:31.960
community is everything, if
you don't have a community

01:06:31.960 --> 01:06:34.820
to express these particular things with,

01:06:34.820 --> 01:06:36.602
then you begin to build it.

01:06:36.602 --> 01:06:40.463
And sometimes, I know for
me, building community,

01:06:41.370 --> 01:06:43.750
I had to let my guard down with people

01:06:43.750 --> 01:06:45.470
that I might've otherwise disagreed

01:06:45.470 --> 01:06:50.470
with in my more arrogant self contained,

01:06:50.640 --> 01:06:54.500
self sufficient, self
sustaining version of myself,

01:06:54.500 --> 01:06:57.260
the more dependent version
of the person that said,

01:06:57.260 --> 01:06:58.093
you know what?

01:06:58.093 --> 01:06:59.596
I don't know everything, I
don't have it all figured out,

01:06:59.596 --> 01:07:02.270
made relationships across the divide.

01:07:02.270 --> 01:07:06.870
And so now I'm having
conversations with a young lady

01:07:06.870 --> 01:07:11.420
who I would not have agreed
with on certain things,

01:07:11.420 --> 01:07:14.360
but I do agree with her on mental health

01:07:14.360 --> 01:07:16.895
or the issues of womanhood.

01:07:16.895 --> 01:07:20.864
And now I'm like, well, what
else can I learn from her?

01:07:20.864 --> 01:07:22.295
I wouldn't have otherwise,

01:07:22.295 --> 01:07:23.128
if I had not had a conversation

01:07:23.128 --> 01:07:25.060
with her because I disagreed so vehemently

01:07:25.060 --> 01:07:26.040
on these particular things.

01:07:26.040 --> 01:07:27.620
And now we're dialoguing more

01:07:27.620 --> 01:07:29.290
and we're becoming good friends.

01:07:29.290 --> 01:07:32.044
And I think that's happened
very consistently for me.

01:07:32.044 --> 01:07:35.450
And I think that's a part
of what happens as well.

01:07:35.450 --> 01:07:36.783
But so yes.

01:07:41.910 --> 01:07:44.900
- [Audience Member] So
I have bipolar disorder

01:07:44.900 --> 01:07:48.216
and I've had a really great
fortune to have medication

01:07:48.216 --> 01:07:52.080
and therapy, and I'm also
a songwriter and I've been

01:07:52.080 --> 01:07:55.086
able to use music to
process a lot of that.

01:07:55.086 --> 01:07:58.481
And my faith has also been
a very instrumental part

01:07:58.481 --> 01:08:00.803
of my recovery.

01:08:01.820 --> 01:08:04.240
I'm also in a band with
a couple other people

01:08:04.240 --> 01:08:05.960
who also have bipolar disorder.

01:08:05.960 --> 01:08:08.343
- Yeah!
(audience member laughs)

01:08:08.343 --> 01:08:11.083
- [Audience Member] And
so of a lot our songs are

01:08:12.350 --> 01:08:15.210
about mental health, about recovery,

01:08:15.210 --> 01:08:17.390
about healing from abuse and trauma,

01:08:17.390 --> 01:08:19.140
recovery from addiction.

01:08:19.140 --> 01:08:23.090
And even with the help,

01:08:23.090 --> 01:08:25.334
I've had moments and experiences

01:08:25.334 --> 01:08:29.920
of just really being baffled.

01:08:29.920 --> 01:08:33.923
I had to resign from my
job teaching to take care

01:08:35.590 --> 01:08:36.530
of my mental health.

01:08:36.530 --> 01:08:40.520
And through that, I also had
a profound sense that God

01:08:40.520 --> 01:08:43.930
was working through my
mental illness to bring me

01:08:43.930 --> 01:08:45.560
in closer relationship with him,

01:08:45.560 --> 01:08:49.970
to bring me into my own purpose

01:08:49.970 --> 01:08:52.410
and my, like as you were saying,

01:08:52.410 --> 01:08:55.017
sort of a truer version of myself.

01:08:55.017 --> 01:08:59.180
And so my question sometimes

01:08:59.180 --> 01:09:00.720
when I'm experiencing something,

01:09:00.720 --> 01:09:03.963
is this God, or is this bipolar?

01:09:05.720 --> 01:09:06.580
- Yes.

01:09:06.580 --> 01:09:07.540
- [Audience Member] Exactly. Yeah.

01:09:07.540 --> 01:09:08.600
And that's been my answer.

01:09:08.600 --> 01:09:11.237
It's often God working
through my mental illness.

01:09:11.237 --> 01:09:14.630
And my question is,

01:09:14.630 --> 01:09:17.750
how can we help people
navigate those spaces?

01:09:17.750 --> 01:09:21.880
I've talked to a lot of
people who don't really have

01:09:21.880 --> 01:09:24.410
a framework or a language to talk

01:09:24.410 --> 01:09:28.134
about God and mental
illness in the same steps

01:09:28.134 --> 01:09:30.150
or in the same conversation.

01:09:30.150 --> 01:09:34.160
People who, at church,
don't hear much about,

01:09:34.160 --> 01:09:36.140
they might not hear a
lot about mental illness.

01:09:36.140 --> 01:09:38.340
And then in mental health settings,

01:09:38.340 --> 01:09:39.900
not hearing much about God.

01:09:39.900 --> 01:09:43.736
So I'm wondering if there
are ways we can help

01:09:43.736 --> 01:09:45.830
people integrate the two?

01:09:45.830 --> 01:09:48.100
I think this conversation
here is a great example

01:09:48.100 --> 01:09:49.180
of one way,

01:09:49.180 --> 01:09:52.070
and I'm wondering what
other ways might look like.

01:09:52.070 --> 01:09:54.170
- That's great. More power to you.

01:09:54.170 --> 01:09:56.503
Love you. Thank you for
your vulnerability too.

01:09:57.781 --> 01:10:00.511
So, long way to answer this question.

01:10:00.511 --> 01:10:05.511
I was in Paris and I was so
excited about being in Paris.

01:10:05.810 --> 01:10:09.270
It was like, dreams, romantic city.

01:10:09.270 --> 01:10:11.840
I can't wait to see everything
and talk to these people.

01:10:11.840 --> 01:10:14.340
It's gonna be great. The
food is gonna be amazing.

01:10:14.340 --> 01:10:19.340
And then I got to Paris and I
couldn't understand anything.

01:10:20.700 --> 01:10:25.470
And no one would accommodate
my American privilege

01:10:25.470 --> 01:10:29.724
and speak English to me.
(audience laughs)

01:10:29.724 --> 01:10:34.400
And I struggled and I hated it.

01:10:34.400 --> 01:10:37.660
And I couldn't read the
menus and I couldn't

01:10:37.660 --> 01:10:39.280
understand where I was going.

01:10:39.280 --> 01:10:41.770
And no one would explain to me anything.

01:10:41.770 --> 01:10:45.130
And I was just so
disappointed and frustrated.

01:10:45.130 --> 01:10:50.130
I just opted to eat at
McDonald's because I was tired.

01:10:50.650 --> 01:10:51.483
Right?

01:10:53.060 --> 01:10:57.220
But I have a friend of mine who's been

01:10:57.220 --> 01:10:59.040
there for a year now.

01:10:59.040 --> 01:11:01.600
And he said, I felt the same way you did.

01:11:01.600 --> 01:11:04.640
But then I wrestled through
all of the complexity

01:11:04.640 --> 01:11:07.940
of it and saw the beauty
in the complexity and now

01:11:07.940 --> 01:11:09.870
I love Paris.

01:11:09.870 --> 01:11:14.050
And I say that to say
this, that many times,

01:11:14.050 --> 01:11:18.980
especially in the faith community,
we, and just in general,

01:11:18.980 --> 01:11:21.100
we struggle with the nuance.

01:11:21.100 --> 01:11:24.187
We struggle with
complexity. We love it easy.

01:11:24.187 --> 01:11:28.120
And there's beauty in complexity
in wrestling with nuance.

01:11:28.120 --> 01:11:30.830
And in many times we're
creating new categories

01:11:30.830 --> 01:11:33.040
for people that they just aren't...

01:11:33.040 --> 01:11:34.010
"Argh! What?

01:11:34.010 --> 01:11:37.480
Mental health, faith, ah, it's too much.

01:11:37.480 --> 01:11:38.955
I can't do it."

01:11:38.955 --> 01:11:43.340
But this is evidence that
that's a category that needs

01:11:43.340 --> 01:11:45.810
to be walked through,

01:11:45.810 --> 01:11:48.280
and it's often difficult and frustrating,

01:11:48.280 --> 01:11:51.180
but when you get to the
other side, it's worth it.

01:11:51.180 --> 01:11:53.210
And so, and I've seen
that time and time again

01:11:53.210 --> 01:11:56.810
in my life that I'm constantly
creating new categories

01:11:56.810 --> 01:12:00.200
and breaking barriers that
people just didn't think

01:12:00.200 --> 01:12:03.090
could exist, but then
people get to see the beauty

01:12:03.090 --> 01:12:05.230
of it once they get on the other side.

01:12:05.230 --> 01:12:07.840
So this is that as you're talking about,

01:12:07.840 --> 01:12:09.230
and I think it's continuing this,

01:12:09.230 --> 01:12:11.461
even though it's difficult and ugly,

01:12:11.461 --> 01:12:14.453
continue pushing through
to create new categories

01:12:14.453 --> 01:12:17.060
and wrestle with nuance
and seeing the beauty

01:12:17.060 --> 01:12:18.650
in the complexity.

01:12:18.650 --> 01:12:19.483
Yeah.

01:12:21.750 --> 01:12:24.520
- [Audience Member] So you
previously talked about how,

01:12:24.520 --> 01:12:28.470
as you got more involved
in social justice,

01:12:28.470 --> 01:12:29.773
you experienced this rejection,

01:12:29.773 --> 01:12:33.760
and I know previously you've
talked about how you had

01:12:33.760 --> 01:12:37.373
to break up with white evangelicalism.

01:12:38.370 --> 01:12:40.630
And I know that for me, personally,

01:12:40.630 --> 01:12:44.740
that breakup has been
freeing and so liberating

01:12:44.740 --> 01:12:46.420
in many ways and has, I think,

01:12:46.420 --> 01:12:49.910
rejuvenated my faith and my
call to ministry in many ways.

01:12:49.910 --> 01:12:53.730
So I'm interested to know
what that breakup did

01:12:53.730 --> 01:12:58.135
for you and how that has
kind of helped you to regain

01:12:58.135 --> 01:13:02.170
that faith in God and
your mental wellbeing?

01:13:02.170 --> 01:13:07.170
- Yeah, I think, when,
in the podcast, you know,

01:13:07.213 --> 01:13:09.340
it was suggested, "It
sounds like you've broken up

01:13:09.340 --> 01:13:11.150
with white evangelicalism."

01:13:11.150 --> 01:13:15.510
And for me, even though
I was still processing

01:13:15.510 --> 01:13:18.630
in that moment, but what
that meant and what it means

01:13:18.630 --> 01:13:23.630
for me more than anything is not believing

01:13:24.200 --> 01:13:28.937
that there's one sect of my faith.

01:13:28.937 --> 01:13:33.340
One group of people within that
has all the answers. Right?

01:13:33.340 --> 01:13:37.540
And I think I just stayed
on one trek and kept

01:13:37.540 --> 01:13:40.370
reading only the people's
books on this trek.

01:13:40.370 --> 01:13:43.609
And I didn't even read these
books because they said

01:13:43.609 --> 01:13:47.050
all the negative things
about these authors.

01:13:47.050 --> 01:13:50.820
And so I never knew what was
wrong about these authors.

01:13:50.820 --> 01:13:52.550
I never understood what was right.

01:13:52.550 --> 01:13:56.070
And I never realized or assumed or came

01:13:56.070 --> 01:13:57.100
to the conclusion that, wait,

01:13:57.100 --> 01:13:59.260
this trek could be wrong, right?

01:13:59.260 --> 01:14:01.030
Like there's things wrong here that are

01:14:01.030 --> 01:14:02.105
not being acknowledged.

01:14:02.105 --> 01:14:03.647
And so for me, that was,

01:14:03.647 --> 01:14:08.647
it was more about saying
I'm not excluding myself

01:14:10.080 --> 01:14:14.930
from these perspectives
globally, historically,

01:14:14.930 --> 01:14:16.818
that had just aren't talked about, right?

01:14:16.818 --> 01:14:19.325
Usually when we talk about church history,

01:14:19.325 --> 01:14:22.073
for whatever reason, it jumps straight

01:14:22.073 --> 01:14:27.073
from Israel to Europe.

01:14:27.700 --> 01:14:30.090
And I don't, for me,
I'm like, wait a minute.

01:14:30.090 --> 01:14:32.850
What about the Ethiopian that left,

01:14:32.850 --> 01:14:36.393
and where's the church, hey,
you know, Asia has a church,

01:14:39.180 --> 01:14:41.880
the Middle East, the
Syriacs, the Coptics and none

01:14:41.880 --> 01:14:43.515
of that stuff's ever mentioned.

01:14:43.515 --> 01:14:45.970
And that's what helped
me realize it's a very

01:14:45.970 --> 01:14:48.760
Eurocentric colonized view

01:14:48.760 --> 01:14:52.610
on Christianity that I was embracing.

01:14:52.610 --> 01:14:54.400
And I said, I'm not gonna do that.

01:14:54.400 --> 01:14:56.300
I'm going to see it globally.

01:14:56.300 --> 01:15:00.020
And that means there's
gonna be perspectives

01:15:00.020 --> 01:15:02.590
that are gonna fight against each other

01:15:02.590 --> 01:15:07.250
but that's okay because I
think God likes us to not

01:15:07.250 --> 01:15:08.220
have all the answers.

01:15:08.220 --> 01:15:10.320
And when any camp says
they have all the answers,

01:15:10.320 --> 01:15:12.420
that's the camp you might
wanna stay away from.

01:15:12.420 --> 01:15:13.347
Just saying.

01:15:18.210 --> 01:15:20.620
- [Audience Member] Thank
you so much, Lecrae.

01:15:20.620 --> 01:15:23.110
And I just wanted to actually respond

01:15:23.110 --> 01:15:26.478
to the previous comment
about other settings

01:15:26.478 --> 01:15:31.478
of the intersection of
mental health and faith.

01:15:32.450 --> 01:15:35.698
And so, even, I can
speak for myself as well

01:15:35.698 --> 01:15:37.600
as a mental health provider.

01:15:37.600 --> 01:15:40.820
And there are several in
the room as well who are

01:15:40.820 --> 01:15:44.150
people of faith, and
from personal experience,

01:15:44.150 --> 01:15:47.510
it's something that
incorporate in the clinic

01:15:47.510 --> 01:15:49.623
practice with my patients,

01:15:50.910 --> 01:15:53.650
I've even prayed with my patients as well.

01:15:53.650 --> 01:15:57.400
And so there are people who
will be open about that,

01:15:57.400 --> 01:15:58.820
not with every patient,

01:15:58.820 --> 01:16:02.370
but there are times where I
may feel led to discuss that.

01:16:02.370 --> 01:16:06.070
And I feel that people can be receptive,

01:16:06.070 --> 01:16:07.550
because they're looking for like

01:16:07.550 --> 01:16:10.930
the bio-psycho-social-spiritual,
that aspect.

01:16:10.930 --> 01:16:14.870
And many people also use
their faith as a coping

01:16:14.870 --> 01:16:16.850
mechanism, coping strategy.

01:16:16.850 --> 01:16:18.860
And so I think that that's also important.

01:16:18.860 --> 01:16:21.880
And I also try to
incorporate that as well.

01:16:21.880 --> 01:16:23.120
So that's another venue.

01:16:23.120 --> 01:16:24.190
And as I mentioned,

01:16:24.190 --> 01:16:27.336
there are several people
in this room who are

01:16:27.336 --> 01:16:30.667
mental health providers
of faith, who practice,

01:16:30.667 --> 01:16:33.332
who are a Christian.

01:16:33.332 --> 01:16:35.130
- Thank you. Thank you.

01:16:35.130 --> 01:16:37.760
And thank you. Keep going.

01:16:37.760 --> 01:16:42.231
Buy some copies of my man's
bipolar band, send 'em out.

01:16:42.231 --> 01:16:45.586
(audience laughs)

01:16:45.586 --> 01:16:48.853
Just before, stream
that, we'll stream that.

01:16:52.680 --> 01:16:54.470
- I really appreciate the conversation.

01:16:54.470 --> 01:16:57.316
And so this is another
opportunity to interact

01:16:57.316 --> 01:16:59.220
with your neighbor.

01:16:59.220 --> 01:17:01.460
And instead of me posing
a question, it's time,

01:17:01.460 --> 01:17:04.097
that comment that you
have, that you wanna say,

01:17:04.097 --> 01:17:07.760
please turn to your neighbor
and share that comment,

01:17:07.760 --> 01:17:10.300
share that insight, just
coming up for you now,

01:17:10.300 --> 01:17:11.210
what you're thinking about,

01:17:11.210 --> 01:17:14.740
what you're holding on to
that is either a struggle

01:17:14.740 --> 01:17:16.750
or maybe a big win for you in this moment.

01:17:16.750 --> 01:17:19.410
Please turn to a person
next to you and share.

01:17:19.410 --> 01:17:21.160
Please leave no one out in sharing.

01:17:24.095 --> 01:17:27.012
(audience murmurs)

01:17:32.840 --> 01:17:33.673
Okay.

01:17:33.673 --> 01:17:34.803
We'll come back together.

01:17:40.460 --> 01:17:45.460
And I wanna ask Lecrae this
final question that I have,

01:17:46.130 --> 01:17:48.903
and also open it up to
four of you to share.

01:17:51.240 --> 01:17:55.648
Looking at culture, how do culture,

01:17:55.648 --> 01:18:00.260
cultural background and
cultural perspectives

01:18:00.260 --> 01:18:02.430
influence your view of yourself

01:18:03.300 --> 01:18:06.480
and how you interact in society?

01:18:06.480 --> 01:18:08.080
So how do culture,

01:18:08.080 --> 01:18:11.200
cultural background and
cultural perspectives

01:18:11.200 --> 01:18:14.410
influence your view of yourself

01:18:14.410 --> 01:18:17.370
and how you interact with society?

01:18:17.370 --> 01:18:18.593
- Wow. Phew.

01:18:24.137 --> 01:18:29.137
When I speak on this, I'm
speaking as a journeyer,

01:18:30.160 --> 01:18:32.170
someone who's on the journey,

01:18:32.170 --> 01:18:35.160
not someone who's reached
the pinnacle and has

01:18:35.160 --> 01:18:36.380
the solutions, right?

01:18:36.380 --> 01:18:39.870
So as I speak about it, I'm not speaking,

01:18:39.870 --> 01:18:42.970
I'm speaking as an expert from experience,

01:18:42.970 --> 01:18:44.750
not an expert from like, oh,

01:18:44.750 --> 01:18:47.860
I've researched this and
I have the answers, right?

01:18:47.860 --> 01:18:51.623
So, but in my experience,

01:18:52.580 --> 01:18:56.550
similar to what my friend here was talking

01:18:56.550 --> 01:19:01.550
about in terms of God
using his being bipolar,

01:19:03.050 --> 01:19:06.393
I think my cultural experiences have been,

01:19:07.330 --> 01:19:09.862
though detrimental and painful,

01:19:09.862 --> 01:19:14.190
have been used to shape
a lot of who I am now.

01:19:14.190 --> 01:19:19.190
And it's been me not being
ashamed of them, but to say, no,

01:19:19.640 --> 01:19:21.500
this is a part of my narrative.

01:19:21.500 --> 01:19:24.330
And narrative is, no one,

01:19:24.330 --> 01:19:27.240
we all learn from narrative,
right? We love narrative.

01:19:27.240 --> 01:19:28.470
We learned from story,

01:19:28.470 --> 01:19:31.240
especially stories of trial and triumph.

01:19:31.240 --> 01:19:34.650
And so no one wants to go to
a "Lord of the Rings" film

01:19:34.650 --> 01:19:39.280
and just see there a kid
who lost his way and found

01:19:39.280 --> 01:19:41.690
the ring and thought the
ring was a part of himself,

01:19:41.690 --> 01:19:44.730
but it wasn't, and then he
found himself and now the end.

01:19:44.730 --> 01:19:47.360
Like we wanna see the
whole thing play out.

01:19:47.360 --> 01:19:51.930
And I think we forget

01:19:51.930 --> 01:19:54.420
that we're in a narrative,

01:19:54.420 --> 01:19:56.060
where our narrative is being written.

01:19:56.060 --> 01:19:57.420
We're a part of a grand narrative.

01:19:57.420 --> 01:20:02.420
And so I feel like culture, in many ways,

01:20:02.880 --> 01:20:06.100
shapes our views on the world,

01:20:06.100 --> 01:20:08.040
and that's why we can
oftentimes need to come

01:20:08.040 --> 01:20:09.530
together with other cultures to like,

01:20:09.530 --> 01:20:12.483
have a complete perspective.

01:20:13.653 --> 01:20:15.226
And sometimes it's negative and sometimes

01:20:15.226 --> 01:20:16.380
it has its benefits.

01:20:16.380 --> 01:20:19.380
I don't think it's all, like,

01:20:19.380 --> 01:20:21.900
I can't look at how I
came up or what I came

01:20:21.900 --> 01:20:26.900
through and think it
is the best or the way.

01:20:27.390 --> 01:20:28.890
And I think that's where the danger lies.

01:20:28.890 --> 01:20:31.610
So another analogy I use,

01:20:31.610 --> 01:20:33.923
I use analogies often
to drive my wife crazy,

01:20:34.826 --> 01:20:37.490
but it's how I communicate,
is if I'm at a baseball game,

01:20:37.490 --> 01:20:38.710
I hate baseball by the way,

01:20:38.710 --> 01:20:41.473
but if I was to be at a baseball game,

01:20:42.790 --> 01:20:47.500
if I'm sitting outfield watching the game,

01:20:47.500 --> 01:20:51.840
I would think I have a great
perspective of the game,

01:20:51.840 --> 01:20:56.050
but then someone's sitting
behind the batter would say, no,

01:20:56.050 --> 01:20:59.401
I have a great perspective
of the whole game.

01:20:59.401 --> 01:21:04.401
And meanwhile, the pitcher
saying, no, I'm in the game.

01:21:06.210 --> 01:21:08.970
I have a great perspective of the game.

01:21:08.970 --> 01:21:11.256
And I think the reality is every one

01:21:11.256 --> 01:21:14.230
of those particular people
have a vantage point,

01:21:14.230 --> 01:21:17.120
but it's the collective that is

01:21:19.100 --> 01:21:21.830
helpful for them to see where they lack

01:21:21.830 --> 01:21:24.320
and to see what other people don't see.

01:21:24.320 --> 01:21:26.598
And I think that's what for me,

01:21:26.598 --> 01:21:28.660
my culture has helped me
to see things that other

01:21:28.660 --> 01:21:29.493
people don't see.

01:21:29.493 --> 01:21:30.440
So when people say oh

01:21:30.440 --> 01:21:32.640
the Black Panthers is a
terrorist group, I say, ah,

01:21:32.640 --> 01:21:36.460
they fed my mother as a
child when she was starving.

01:21:36.460 --> 01:21:41.297
So various perspectives. So yeah.

01:21:41.297 --> 01:21:42.130
- Thank you. Thank you.

01:21:44.130 --> 01:21:46.337
Who would like to share?
Oh, you have a mic...

01:21:46.337 --> 01:21:48.880
- [Audience Member] I have a
question. It's actually, yeah.

01:21:48.880 --> 01:21:51.260
My question really just has to do with,

01:21:51.260 --> 01:21:53.280
you mentioned that like you like joined

01:21:53.280 --> 01:21:54.880
these movements and suddenly like,

01:21:54.880 --> 01:21:57.350
all these ratings are going
down and all this tracks,

01:21:57.350 --> 01:22:00.110
and then you put forward, like,

01:22:00.110 --> 01:22:02.012
I'll find you the track saying that,

01:22:02.012 --> 01:22:03.300
it's lit by the way.

01:22:03.300 --> 01:22:05.657
Like, I'm such a huge, I don't get music,

01:22:05.657 --> 01:22:07.260
but my question is,

01:22:07.260 --> 01:22:09.560
how then did you bring
yourself around to learn

01:22:09.560 --> 01:22:12.370
to trust people again, and
to learn to love again?

01:22:12.370 --> 01:22:14.430
Because you mentioned how important, like,

01:22:14.430 --> 01:22:16.440
the approval of people was for you.

01:22:16.440 --> 01:22:18.360
So going through that period of rejection,

01:22:18.360 --> 01:22:21.350
how do you love people authentically

01:22:21.350 --> 01:22:23.020
even after what they've done to you?

01:22:23.020 --> 01:22:23.853
Oh yeah.

01:22:23.853 --> 01:22:27.770
- Oh, that's good. That's a
really good, that took time.

01:22:27.770 --> 01:22:29.420
I'm not gonna lie to
you that it took time.

01:22:29.420 --> 01:22:33.570
It's, it was a season of just like,

01:22:33.570 --> 01:22:37.480
you know, yeah,

01:22:37.480 --> 01:22:39.870
just certain authors,
my stomach, I'll throw

01:22:39.870 --> 01:22:41.810
that book in the trash.
You know what I mean?

01:22:41.810 --> 01:22:46.497
Just, but part of it is,
as you mentioned, like,

01:22:46.497 --> 01:22:48.080
"I'll Find You", "I'll Find
You" is the biggest song

01:22:48.080 --> 01:22:50.730
I've ever put out in my career,

01:22:50.730 --> 01:22:54.487
in the darkest time of my life.

01:22:54.487 --> 01:22:58.530
And I think that's part of the lesson,

01:22:58.530 --> 01:23:00.410
that God said, well, good.

01:23:00.410 --> 01:23:04.410
You saw my acceptance, not other people's.

01:23:04.410 --> 01:23:07.830
And look what did in that process, right?

01:23:07.830 --> 01:23:09.479
Oh, you were fighting for acceptance.

01:23:09.479 --> 01:23:10.790
You hit a ceiling,

01:23:10.790 --> 01:23:12.800
but when you stopped
fighting for acceptance

01:23:12.800 --> 01:23:15.330
and just were authentically yourself,

01:23:15.330 --> 01:23:16.690
look at what happened.

01:23:16.690 --> 01:23:18.800
And people are drawn
to authenticity anyway.

01:23:18.800 --> 01:23:21.510
So you liberated a lot more
people than you would've

01:23:21.510 --> 01:23:22.920
ever met, just so that was part one.

01:23:22.920 --> 01:23:25.970
And then trusting and loving people again,

01:23:25.970 --> 01:23:30.540
it's just really hard to be hopeful

01:23:30.540 --> 01:23:33.210
and hateful at the same time.

01:23:33.210 --> 01:23:35.690
Right? It's really hard
to be hopeful and hateful.

01:23:35.690 --> 01:23:39.350
And so, as my hope increased,

01:23:39.350 --> 01:23:41.310
my love increased for other people,

01:23:41.310 --> 01:23:44.050
because as I have a hope in and of myself,

01:23:44.050 --> 01:23:49.050
it's just really hard for
me to just look at someone

01:23:49.160 --> 01:23:51.500
else and say, "They just make me sick."

01:23:51.500 --> 01:23:52.333
Right? You know?

01:23:52.333 --> 01:23:54.750
So the trust was a process.

01:23:54.750 --> 01:23:55.919
It didn't happen overnight,

01:23:55.919 --> 01:24:00.180
but as I took time for
self care to grow...

01:24:00.180 --> 01:24:02.790
I found more security. I
understood myself more.

01:24:02.790 --> 01:24:04.800
I filled in some gaps, right?

01:24:04.800 --> 01:24:08.590
In my own understanding
about faith, about...

01:24:08.590 --> 01:24:13.197
I read a lot of books
from all over the world

01:24:13.197 --> 01:24:15.780
from different authors that
I wouldn't have otherwise

01:24:15.780 --> 01:24:17.550
read and gaps got filled in.

01:24:17.550 --> 01:24:21.290
And then I was hopeful and I
was like, oh, this is great.

01:24:21.290 --> 01:24:23.830
It's like, well, I'm not
mad at y'all no more,

01:24:23.830 --> 01:24:24.740
I feel bad for y'all,

01:24:24.740 --> 01:24:25.729
'cause I know things that y'all don't.

01:24:25.729 --> 01:24:26.920
It's pretty bad.

01:24:26.920 --> 01:24:28.397
Y'all don't know this,
you know what I mean?

01:24:28.397 --> 01:24:30.033
And so it was a process.

01:24:36.460 --> 01:24:37.293
If you didn't hear her,

01:24:37.293 --> 01:24:42.070
she was asking for strategies
to fight what feels like

01:24:42.913 --> 01:24:45.913
a continual state of brokenness.

01:24:47.090 --> 01:24:50.810
And my youngest son

01:24:51.860 --> 01:24:54.433
was a very, very pitiful child.

01:24:56.090 --> 01:24:59.650
I mean, just being honest,
he couldn't dress himself.

01:24:59.650 --> 01:25:01.550
He used the bathroom
on himself constantly.

01:25:01.550 --> 01:25:03.610
He couldn't feed himself,
just really pitiful.

01:25:03.610 --> 01:25:04.943
It was sad, man.

01:25:06.110 --> 01:25:11.110
And, but I wasn't frustrated
at him, because he was my son.

01:25:12.210 --> 01:25:17.210
And because even though
performance wise, he was a mess,

01:25:19.620 --> 01:25:24.020
positionally, he was my son
and he was gonna be great.

01:25:24.020 --> 01:25:25.860
And I'll change his diapers for him.

01:25:25.860 --> 01:25:27.903
And I'll feed him in this process.

01:25:27.903 --> 01:25:32.903
And I think we, in a state
of perpetual brokenness,

01:25:33.220 --> 01:25:36.830
sometimes our performance seems poor,

01:25:36.830 --> 01:25:38.870
and that's okay sometimes,

01:25:38.870 --> 01:25:43.230
because it reminds us of our positioning.

01:25:43.230 --> 01:25:46.490
And I think the strategy for me in a place

01:25:46.490 --> 01:25:50.246
where I feel like I just can't get past

01:25:50.246 --> 01:25:53.653
how ugly everything feels
and looks around me.

01:25:55.370 --> 01:25:57.090
It made me that much more dependent.

01:25:57.090 --> 01:26:02.090
And then I began to be
disciplined in dependency,

01:26:02.160 --> 01:26:04.130
because I didn't have a choice, right?

01:26:04.130 --> 01:26:08.678
If you've ever been sick or broken a limb,

01:26:08.678 --> 01:26:12.510
you learn how to become
dependent on other people

01:26:12.510 --> 01:26:15.950
or you learn how to function
in a new kind of way.

01:26:15.950 --> 01:26:18.540
And that's what happened
to me in that state

01:26:18.540 --> 01:26:20.170
is just constant dependency.

01:26:20.170 --> 01:26:22.669
Dependency on God, dependency on friends,

01:26:22.669 --> 01:26:26.400
kind of throwing myself on
the train tracks of faith.

01:26:26.400 --> 01:26:29.220
And what that means is even
if it seems like black ink

01:26:29.220 --> 01:26:30.867
on white pages, I'm just gonna read it.

01:26:30.867 --> 01:26:32.597
I'm gonna read it. I'm
gonna keep reading it.

01:26:32.597 --> 01:26:36.213
I'm gonna keep reading it
until it gets in me, you know?

01:26:36.213 --> 01:26:39.930
And I'm gonna put myself
around people that I know mean

01:26:39.930 --> 01:26:41.720
me well and not harm,

01:26:41.720 --> 01:26:43.850
even if I don't feel
like being around them.

01:26:43.850 --> 01:26:47.095
And I'm gonna fight to
become a dependent person

01:26:47.095 --> 01:26:48.746
until it's like, oh wow.

01:26:48.746 --> 01:26:52.240
I am out of this position. I'm healed.

01:26:52.240 --> 01:26:53.073
Right?

01:26:54.260 --> 01:26:55.093
- Thank you.

01:26:56.380 --> 01:26:58.520
- [Audience Member] Hi.
Thanks for being here.

01:26:58.520 --> 01:27:01.790
My question is sort of
on the flip side of kind

01:27:01.790 --> 01:27:05.250
of dealing with haters
and people who don't

01:27:05.250 --> 01:27:06.600
necessarily agree with you.

01:27:07.960 --> 01:27:10.790
Kind of in your process
of rise to success,

01:27:10.790 --> 01:27:14.970
how have you managed to stay humble?

01:27:14.970 --> 01:27:18.540
And you're also talking
about a big part of mental

01:27:18.540 --> 01:27:22.660
health being the communities
that you kind of go back

01:27:22.660 --> 01:27:24.332
to for support.

01:27:24.332 --> 01:27:29.332
Has that sort of rise to fame
made you alienated at all,

01:27:29.900 --> 01:27:31.530
felt alienated like you can't,

01:27:31.530 --> 01:27:36.400
like sort of people

01:27:36.400 --> 01:27:37.233
can't maybe relate

01:27:37.233 --> 01:27:39.023
to you in the way that they used to?

01:27:40.880 --> 01:27:43.421
- First of all, I don't know
who told you I was humble.

01:27:43.421 --> 01:27:46.254
(audience laughs)

01:27:48.120 --> 01:27:53.120
I'm not, I'm constantly
practicing the art of humility.

01:27:54.260 --> 01:27:59.143
It's a lifetime struggle.
So feel free to join me.

01:28:00.360 --> 01:28:05.360
Yeah. So humility is just daily,
like reminding yourself...

01:28:06.560 --> 01:28:09.570
I read a book on it every year.

01:28:09.570 --> 01:28:11.570
I'll find a different one if I need to,

01:28:11.570 --> 01:28:16.030
but there's just a lot of
different perspectives,

01:28:16.030 --> 01:28:17.970
as a part of it is like
just reminding myself

01:28:17.970 --> 01:28:21.620
of how big God is. And then
you are all, oh, I'm small.

01:28:21.620 --> 01:28:23.513
That's good. This is a good reminder.

01:28:25.450 --> 01:28:28.520
But yeah. And keeping
close friends around me.

01:28:28.520 --> 01:28:33.193
So, to answer your question, yes,

01:28:35.750 --> 01:28:38.870
I do keep close, day one,

01:28:38.870 --> 01:28:41.110
original friends who know me as me,

01:28:41.110 --> 01:28:44.860
not me who rose to whatever around

01:28:44.860 --> 01:28:46.410
because they keep me humble.

01:28:46.410 --> 01:28:49.797
It's very hard for them to
see the difference that like,

01:28:49.797 --> 01:28:52.630
"Why do they want you to
autograph, that's stupid?"

01:28:52.630 --> 01:28:54.650
You know what I mean?
(audience laughs)

01:28:54.650 --> 01:28:57.283
So I keep those people around.

01:29:00.010 --> 01:29:03.940
But then yes is hard because it takes them

01:29:03.940 --> 01:29:06.030
a while to breathe that air, right?

01:29:06.030 --> 01:29:08.290
It takes them a while
and they have to adjust.

01:29:08.290 --> 01:29:10.150
Oprah said it once, she said, man,

01:29:10.150 --> 01:29:13.419
my friends have struggled
to breathe my air.

01:29:13.419 --> 01:29:17.176
And I was like, what is she talking about?

01:29:17.176 --> 01:29:18.950
You know what I mean?
I just, I don't get it.

01:29:18.950 --> 01:29:22.040
But then I did understand
it after a season.

01:29:22.040 --> 01:29:23.113
And it's kinda like,

01:29:24.830 --> 01:29:29.680
I'm leaving Jimmy Fallon
and my friends are like, yo,

01:29:29.680 --> 01:29:32.090
let's stand outside and look
around and take pictures.

01:29:32.090 --> 01:29:35.020
And I'm like, I can't do that. Right?

01:29:35.020 --> 01:29:35.890
Like I'm...

01:29:35.890 --> 01:29:37.900
There's, you know, there's
just different little things,

01:29:37.900 --> 01:29:40.330
little nuances that
they have to learn like,

01:29:40.330 --> 01:29:41.730
oh, that's right.

01:29:41.730 --> 01:29:43.352
Your life sucks.

01:29:43.352 --> 01:29:46.185
(audience laughs)

01:29:47.320 --> 01:29:49.863
But then over time they,

01:29:50.820 --> 01:29:53.050
I tell them and they
understand, they grasp it.

01:29:53.050 --> 01:29:54.670
And then they serve me in that capacity

01:29:54.670 --> 01:29:55.820
and they start trying to protect me.

01:29:55.820 --> 01:29:57.090
And those are the people
that I want around,

01:29:57.090 --> 01:29:58.380
people who don't try to take advantage

01:29:58.380 --> 01:29:59.820
of the unique opportunities I have,

01:29:59.820 --> 01:30:03.030
but wanna protect me
from other random stuff.

01:30:03.030 --> 01:30:04.023
So yeah.

01:30:05.100 --> 01:30:05.933
- Thank you.

01:30:07.850 --> 01:30:09.443
- [Audience Member] Thank you so much.

01:30:10.750 --> 01:30:14.460
I am just in wonder,

01:30:14.460 --> 01:30:17.760
'cause there's so many
elements that I wanna talk

01:30:17.760 --> 01:30:19.940
about for like three
hours and there's, like,

01:30:19.940 --> 01:30:21.723
just keep track on top of them.

01:30:22.570 --> 01:30:24.920
But one of the things that,

01:30:24.920 --> 01:30:27.880
a word that keeps coming
through this presentation,

01:30:27.880 --> 01:30:31.060
through this town hall is wholeness.

01:30:31.060 --> 01:30:34.650
I keep thinking returning
the brokenness and wholeness

01:30:34.650 --> 01:30:37.110
and wholeness in brokenness.

01:30:37.110 --> 01:30:40.310
But in this cultural section,

01:30:40.310 --> 01:30:43.110
I feel as though a lot of times

01:30:46.870 --> 01:30:48.700
I've also segmented

01:30:48.700 --> 01:30:52.070
parts of myself and
ignored parts of myself.

01:30:52.070 --> 01:30:54.260
And I feel as though I'm
in an environment here

01:30:54.260 --> 01:30:58.193
at Yale, which encourages
me to walk on my hands,

01:30:59.030 --> 01:31:03.480
and I get strong walking
on my hands, thinking,

01:31:03.480 --> 01:31:08.257
not feeling, being able to analyze

01:31:08.257 --> 01:31:10.123
and not empathize.

01:31:11.410 --> 01:31:16.410
And I wanna ask, how do you stop yourself,

01:31:16.870 --> 01:31:20.350
when you find yourself
continuing to walk on your hands,

01:31:20.350 --> 01:31:23.700
continuing to go back to
that self that's only a part

01:31:23.700 --> 01:31:25.203
of you and not the whole you?

01:31:27.660 --> 01:31:29.220
- You already accomplished the first step

01:31:29.220 --> 01:31:32.710
as you acknowledged,
you're plowing through,

01:31:32.710 --> 01:31:34.890
you acknowledged that you're
walking on your hands.

01:31:34.890 --> 01:31:36.520
Right?

01:31:36.520 --> 01:31:39.600
I think it should be an encouragement

01:31:39.600 --> 01:31:41.800
to you that when you said that,

01:31:41.800 --> 01:31:43.647
how many people in this room groaned like,

01:31:43.647 --> 01:31:47.660
"Ooh," you're not alone.

01:31:47.660 --> 01:31:50.740
There's a room full of people
who are like, "I agree."

01:31:50.740 --> 01:31:54.250
And I think you foster that
community and you become.

01:31:54.250 --> 01:31:55.763
Look at me, man. I'm wearing pants.

01:31:55.763 --> 01:31:59.832
You see these pants?
These don't make sense.

01:31:59.832 --> 01:32:03.120
(audience laughs)

01:32:03.120 --> 01:32:06.013
These don't make sense, but I don't care.

01:32:08.130 --> 01:32:11.857
'Cause I like 'em. So I'm gonna wear them.

01:32:11.857 --> 01:32:16.203
And my word, my value is not diminished.

01:32:16.203 --> 01:32:19.900
Some people are saying,
"What is he wearing?"

01:32:19.900 --> 01:32:22.710
Some people are saying,
yeah, he's a good artist.

01:32:22.710 --> 01:32:27.710
It's what the artists do-
(audience laughs)

01:32:28.375 --> 01:32:31.350
But I'm like, I wanna wear 'em.

01:32:31.350 --> 01:32:32.183
So I wear 'em.

01:32:32.183 --> 01:32:35.313
And I think it's that,

01:32:37.370 --> 01:32:42.370
that's what a prestigious
institution builds.

01:32:43.100 --> 01:32:45.660
Builds that kind of character,

01:32:45.660 --> 01:32:48.473
that kind of authenticity that says,

01:32:50.330 --> 01:32:55.003
I'm who I am and who I am is accepted.

01:32:57.630 --> 01:33:00.000
And you have a community here that accepts

01:33:00.000 --> 01:33:02.980
you and you're not fighting
for their acceptance.

01:33:02.980 --> 01:33:04.910
You're not fighting for
anybody's acceptance.

01:33:04.910 --> 01:33:07.557
You're being who you
authentically should be.

01:33:07.557 --> 01:33:09.500
That's what success is, right?

01:33:09.500 --> 01:33:14.180
And so I think it's resting
in those moans that you heard,

01:33:14.180 --> 01:33:16.570
it's resting in all the people that you.

01:33:16.570 --> 01:33:19.210
'Cause truthfully that's how
everybody's feeling, bro.

01:33:19.210 --> 01:33:20.290
That's, they're all feeling that.

01:33:20.290 --> 01:33:22.690
They're just afraid to show it.

01:33:22.690 --> 01:33:24.447
They're afraid to say,

01:33:24.447 --> 01:33:26.940
"I wanna be accepted by
everybody so I'm not gonna be

01:33:26.940 --> 01:33:29.410
my real self."
(audience laughs)

01:33:29.410 --> 01:33:32.000
They're afraid to say
that, but it's the truth.

01:33:32.000 --> 01:33:33.910
Everyone's in a performance trap.

01:33:33.910 --> 01:33:36.959
And we were talking earlier and I said,

01:33:36.959 --> 01:33:39.310
the thing about you all here

01:33:39.310 --> 01:33:41.870
is you're so exceptionally gifted

01:33:41.870 --> 01:33:45.409
and you're in an arena where
all the emphasis is put

01:33:45.409 --> 01:33:50.143
on how exceptionally gifted
you are academically.

01:33:50.143 --> 01:33:53.470
But that doesn't mean
you're whole emotionally,

01:33:53.470 --> 01:33:55.156
mentally, spiritually.

01:33:55.156 --> 01:33:56.570
Right? Does it?

01:33:56.570 --> 01:33:59.020
Why do they put a gym on campus?

01:33:59.020 --> 01:34:02.283
'Cause they know. (indistinct)

01:34:05.830 --> 01:34:08.130
So I'm just, it's okay.

01:34:08.130 --> 01:34:09.860
Acknowledge it, live in it, walk in it,

01:34:09.860 --> 01:34:13.920
breathe it in and own it, man, own that.

01:34:13.920 --> 01:34:15.610
Own it, "I'm tired of walking on my hands.

01:34:15.610 --> 01:34:18.430
I'm not gonna do it." And
there's other people around you.

01:34:18.430 --> 01:34:20.920
That's how movements get started.
It's like a little video.

01:34:20.920 --> 01:34:24.620
It's funny. It's like a guy
on a hillside, you know?

01:34:24.620 --> 01:34:26.830
And he's like dancing
crazy in front of a bunch

01:34:26.830 --> 01:34:28.731
of people just chilling
and looking at him like,

01:34:28.731 --> 01:34:29.840
what is he doing?

01:34:29.840 --> 01:34:31.760
But then the second
guy comes up and starts

01:34:31.760 --> 01:34:32.950
dancing with them.

01:34:32.950 --> 01:34:35.790
And then that second guy
is the person that starts

01:34:35.790 --> 01:34:36.720
the movement, right?

01:34:36.720 --> 01:34:38.518
It's all it takes is one other
person to stand beside you.

01:34:38.518 --> 01:34:41.050
And after the second guy
in the third and the fourth

01:34:41.050 --> 01:34:41.883
and the fifth and everyone else,

01:34:41.883 --> 01:34:43.950
like I don't wanna be left
out, I don't wanna be left out.

01:34:43.950 --> 01:34:44.783
They wanted to get up.

01:34:44.783 --> 01:34:46.780
And they were so afraid of
what everyone else would say.

01:34:46.780 --> 01:34:47.900
The group thing happened.

01:34:47.900 --> 01:34:51.257
But the beauty of it
was somebody had to be

01:34:51.257 --> 01:34:55.710
the first person to say, I
felt like dancing, I'm dancing.

01:34:55.710 --> 01:34:58.850
So, more power to you,
you heard the moans.

01:34:58.850 --> 01:34:59.823
We wish you, man.

01:35:03.770 --> 01:35:04.800
- [Audience Member] Name's J. Maurice.

01:35:04.800 --> 01:35:06.380
And I wanted to thank you.

01:35:06.380 --> 01:35:09.240
I had to call my children
and ask them who Lecrae was,

01:35:09.240 --> 01:35:12.430
so I wasn't sure, but they
told me to come anyway.

01:35:12.430 --> 01:35:16.010
But one of the things that you mentioned,

01:35:16.010 --> 01:35:18.130
and I think this is part of
the experience that we have,

01:35:18.130 --> 01:35:22.307
is that we talk about the
church and going to the church.

01:35:22.307 --> 01:35:25.280
And I think many of us
have gone to the church

01:35:25.280 --> 01:35:28.110
and have felt neglected and abandoned.

01:35:28.110 --> 01:35:30.210
And so one of the things you've done here,

01:35:31.810 --> 01:35:34.170
you talk about transparency
and vulnerability.

01:35:34.170 --> 01:35:36.826
And when you look at the
New Testament, you came

01:35:36.826 --> 01:35:39.200
to Christ as you were.

01:35:39.200 --> 01:35:42.080
I think so often where
we're afraid to do that.

01:35:42.080 --> 01:35:44.450
And you talked about
authenticity and intentionality.

01:35:44.450 --> 01:35:47.080
So I appreciate the fact
and now I'm asking him

01:35:47.080 --> 01:35:49.270
what can I get from this?

01:35:49.270 --> 01:35:53.280
But it's a reminder of me that
learning process is ongoing.

01:35:53.280 --> 01:35:57.440
And this looks like a church
and we're having church,

01:35:57.440 --> 01:35:59.950
because there's a level of
openness and transparency

01:35:59.950 --> 01:36:01.970
that we can always have in the church.

01:36:01.970 --> 01:36:02.830
And so I think,

01:36:02.830 --> 01:36:05.830
as we take this away and I do
a lot of work with leaders,

01:36:05.830 --> 01:36:07.560
we don't have this conversation,

01:36:07.560 --> 01:36:08.735
because there's a sense of,

01:36:08.735 --> 01:36:12.260
I can't let other folks know where I am.

01:36:12.260 --> 01:36:15.300
And you also talk about our
community and our cultures.

01:36:15.300 --> 01:36:17.570
A lot of our culture and
committees are surrounded

01:36:17.570 --> 01:36:20.540
by violence, but we live in that space.

01:36:20.540 --> 01:36:22.160
And so it becomes a commonplace thing.

01:36:22.160 --> 01:36:24.140
But once you've created
here as a sense of...

01:36:24.140 --> 01:36:26.410
And so for me, this is church.

01:36:26.410 --> 01:36:28.820
This is what church is
supposed to be about.

01:36:28.820 --> 01:36:31.390
And so I think that's
as we in here believe

01:36:34.090 --> 01:36:36.730
to trust, to love, to
reach out to one another,

01:36:36.730 --> 01:36:40.690
because people are hurting
and you've said that.

01:36:40.690 --> 01:36:42.650
And if we can create that sense of church,

01:36:42.650 --> 01:36:44.330
I think where we are, it's important.

01:36:44.330 --> 01:36:47.220
But I'm curious in terms
of when did you come

01:36:47.220 --> 01:36:51.587
into that space where
you realize your faith

01:36:51.587 --> 01:36:52.690
and what that meant to you?

01:36:52.690 --> 01:36:55.390
What, was the place or
time when you just stepped

01:36:55.390 --> 01:36:56.352
into that space and said, you know what?

01:36:56.352 --> 01:36:59.010
I'm gonna be me. This is
who God created me to be.

01:36:59.010 --> 01:37:00.587
And if anyone else has problem with it,

01:37:00.587 --> 01:37:01.870
that's their problem.

01:37:01.870 --> 01:37:04.200
When did you come into that
space was like, you know what?

01:37:04.200 --> 01:37:05.163
This is who I am.

01:37:06.650 --> 01:37:11.010
- Yeah. I think over
the last two years is...

01:37:11.010 --> 01:37:13.653
I've always acknowledged my faith,

01:37:15.320 --> 01:37:19.090
since I became a believer,

01:37:19.090 --> 01:37:24.090
but I think over the last two
years, it wasn't group think,

01:37:25.030 --> 01:37:29.530
it wasn't going along with everyone's,

01:37:29.530 --> 01:37:31.800
what people will pat me on the back for.

01:37:31.800 --> 01:37:35.040
And because I grew up
fatherless and I'm not saying

01:37:35.040 --> 01:37:39.480
that's the reason for everyone's
situation, but for me,

01:37:39.480 --> 01:37:42.970
that was the ingredient for
my desire for acceptance

01:37:42.970 --> 01:37:46.670
and approval, was because I
didn't get that affirmation.

01:37:46.670 --> 01:37:48.697
Like no one ever said,

01:37:48.697 --> 01:37:50.400
"Son, I wanna tell you did a good job."

01:37:50.400 --> 01:37:54.805
I never heard that. So I
longed to hear it somewhere.

01:37:54.805 --> 01:37:58.780
And it wasn't until I said,

01:37:58.780 --> 01:38:02.323
oh, God already said that.

01:38:03.167 --> 01:38:05.810
You know what I mean? God
already said, "I love you."

01:38:05.810 --> 01:38:08.917
And I'm proud of you, and
keep going, keep trusting me.

01:38:08.917 --> 01:38:11.817
And you don't have it all
together, but that's okay. I do.

01:38:12.720 --> 01:38:15.530
Oh, that's freeing. That's liberating.

01:38:15.530 --> 01:38:18.343
Well, let me just be
who I'm supposed to be.

01:38:18.343 --> 01:38:21.350
And I think, unfortunately for me,

01:38:21.350 --> 01:38:24.920
I had to go through hell to get there.

01:38:24.920 --> 01:38:28.513
Right? I had to be pushed
to the edge of insanity.

01:38:32.270 --> 01:38:34.510
So I was left with no choice.
I don't want that for anybody.

01:38:34.510 --> 01:38:38.640
I don't want anybody to get to
the ledge and be like, well,

01:38:38.640 --> 01:38:41.550
I hopefully that people will, like I said,

01:38:41.550 --> 01:38:44.450
be broken before you are
broken, you know what I mean?

01:38:44.450 --> 01:38:47.040
That's what my hope would
be for everybody. So...

01:38:49.350 --> 01:38:50.320
- Yeah. One more.

01:38:53.210 --> 01:38:54.880
- [Audience Member] Hello.

01:38:54.880 --> 01:38:58.100
So I guess my question is

01:38:59.440 --> 01:39:01.530
I was just speaking with my step son

01:39:01.530 --> 01:39:06.120
about my challenges of
balancing my work life

01:39:06.120 --> 01:39:07.800
and my personal life.

01:39:07.800 --> 01:39:11.930
And so my main challenge is
that I feel like everybody

01:39:11.930 --> 01:39:14.550
has a need for me.

01:39:14.550 --> 01:39:15.533
And I have to be,

01:39:15.533 --> 01:39:18.369
I have to wear a million
hats and I have to be

01:39:18.369 --> 01:39:20.420
100 different places.

01:39:20.420 --> 01:39:24.700
And how do I be able to
take care of my own self

01:39:24.700 --> 01:39:27.410
and take time out for
myself when I have so many

01:39:27.410 --> 01:39:30.403
people that have all
these demands on myself?

01:39:31.970 --> 01:39:36.970
- It's good. Every scenario is different.

01:39:37.110 --> 01:39:41.403
So I can't speak to your
specific circumstances, right?

01:39:42.870 --> 01:39:44.740
I can speak in generalities
and I can speak

01:39:44.740 --> 01:39:45.640
about my experience.

01:39:45.640 --> 01:39:48.690
And for me, what I've had to
do is, like I said before,

01:39:48.690 --> 01:39:53.690
I think sometimes busyness means

01:39:54.840 --> 01:39:56.920
we're out of control, right?

01:39:56.920 --> 01:39:59.604
So it's taking back some of the control,

01:39:59.604 --> 01:40:03.340
and a large part of that, for me,

01:40:03.340 --> 01:40:07.530
has been creating,

01:40:07.530 --> 01:40:12.080
prioritizing space for rest,

01:40:12.080 --> 01:40:16.330
prioritizing space for self care.

01:40:16.330 --> 01:40:17.990
It's not me being selfish.

01:40:17.990 --> 01:40:21.420
It's me actually trying to
care about everyone else.

01:40:21.420 --> 01:40:24.742
And if I'm gonna lose my
mind, I'm no good for y'all.

01:40:24.742 --> 01:40:26.870
So I prioritize it,

01:40:26.870 --> 01:40:29.640
and we prioritize lots of things, right?

01:40:29.640 --> 01:40:31.810
If you're involved in any
kind of extracurricular,

01:40:31.810 --> 01:40:34.350
you somehow found a way to
prioritize that and to put

01:40:34.350 --> 01:40:36.319
that in your life.

01:40:36.319 --> 01:40:40.260
If you're caught up on any Netflix series,

01:40:40.260 --> 01:40:45.003
you've prioritized that to
make that a part of your life.

01:40:45.890 --> 01:40:50.670
So it's prioritizing
those breaks in my life.

01:40:50.670 --> 01:40:52.840
And it's putting them on a calendar saying

01:40:52.840 --> 01:40:53.820
you can't touch it.

01:40:53.820 --> 01:40:57.551
Nothing can touch this.
Oh, what are you doing?

01:40:57.551 --> 01:41:01.500
I'm walking through a graveyard
in the middle of the day,

01:41:01.500 --> 01:41:03.920
'cause nobody will talk to me.

01:41:03.920 --> 01:41:05.682
And if it's weird, I don't care.

01:41:05.682 --> 01:41:07.430
(audience laughs)

01:41:07.430 --> 01:41:09.602
But nothing can touch that.

01:41:09.602 --> 01:41:14.602
I might as well be at the
hospital visiting my sick mother,

01:41:15.450 --> 01:41:16.897
you can't touch that time.

01:41:16.897 --> 01:41:19.670
And so it's been for me
creating those spaces

01:41:19.670 --> 01:41:21.090
where I'm like, if I don't get this,

01:41:21.090 --> 01:41:22.410
I'm gonna lose my mind.

01:41:22.410 --> 01:41:26.420
So I've gotta create this
space that just has to happen.

01:41:26.420 --> 01:41:28.810
And so even with...

01:41:31.510 --> 01:41:34.197
I give my wife Tuesdays off.

01:41:34.197 --> 01:41:38.140
So Tuesdays I'm like, go,
live life. I got them.

01:41:38.140 --> 01:41:41.800
Everything. Don't even come
home from sunup to sundown.

01:41:41.800 --> 01:41:46.052
I don't, you don't have
any responsibilities.

01:41:46.052 --> 01:41:47.010
(audience laughs)

01:41:47.010 --> 01:41:48.924
Like, you know what I'm saying? Like...

01:41:48.924 --> 01:41:50.303
No, literally she'll come home

01:41:50.303 --> 01:41:54.010
in the middle of the day
and the house is on fire

01:41:54.010 --> 01:41:55.844
and I got kids everywhere and stuff.

01:41:55.844 --> 01:41:57.990
And then they'll be like, "Mommy."

01:41:57.990 --> 01:41:59.910
She says, I don't exist, bye.

01:41:59.910 --> 01:42:02.350
And she'll just like walk
through the house, but it's like,

01:42:02.350 --> 01:42:05.450
but we created that
space, where it's, like,

01:42:05.450 --> 01:42:06.967
nothing, nothing can stop it.

01:42:06.967 --> 01:42:09.990
And so no matter what opportunity
or whatever, it's like,

01:42:09.990 --> 01:42:11.420
this is blocked.

01:42:11.420 --> 01:42:12.430
Right? You know what I mean?

01:42:12.430 --> 01:42:15.960
And so that's what I
would say is just create,

01:42:15.960 --> 01:42:19.370
like prioritize, whatever
it needs to be prioritized

01:42:19.370 --> 01:42:24.360
for you to feed your soul,
feed your mind, your emotion,

01:42:24.360 --> 01:42:25.760
your health, whatever it is.

01:42:27.373 --> 01:42:31.650
- I'm gonna help us to
transition into ongoing work

01:42:31.650 --> 01:42:33.500
and ongoing conversation.

01:42:33.500 --> 01:42:36.830
And I will ask Lecrae to give
us thoughts to send us off.

01:42:36.830 --> 01:42:39.360
And I will invite my university chaplain,

01:42:39.360 --> 01:42:41.050
Sharon Kugler, up to close.

01:42:41.050 --> 01:42:45.040
Lecrae mentioned, that
is the second person

01:42:45.040 --> 01:42:46.389
who stands beside the first person

01:42:46.389 --> 01:42:48.420
that starts the movement.

01:42:48.420 --> 01:42:52.259
I recognize tonight that
we are the second person.

01:42:52.259 --> 01:42:57.259
And some of you in this room
are the first person. Who...

01:42:58.150 --> 01:43:00.540
You've been having these conversations.

01:43:00.540 --> 01:43:03.490
You've been doing this work.
You may have created resources.

01:43:03.490 --> 01:43:06.640
You may have created space for this type.

01:43:06.640 --> 01:43:09.224
And one of the things I've learned

01:43:09.224 --> 01:43:13.580
about Yale for my time here
is that I don't know all

01:43:13.580 --> 01:43:17.915
that I should know about
what opportunities exist

01:43:17.915 --> 01:43:21.433
and who else may be
interested in something

01:43:21.433 --> 01:43:22.750
that I'm interested in.

01:43:22.750 --> 01:43:25.590
There is some comments
tonight that I think some

01:43:25.590 --> 01:43:27.760
of you may have heard and said,

01:43:27.760 --> 01:43:30.500
I'm actually doing well in that area.

01:43:30.500 --> 01:43:35.090
And I'm willing to share
how I'm having victory

01:43:35.090 --> 01:43:36.920
in that area.

01:43:36.920 --> 01:43:38.520
And there's some of you
tonight who might've

01:43:38.520 --> 01:43:40.400
heard something and said, you know what?

01:43:40.400 --> 01:43:44.700
This has been a pain in
my existence for a while,

01:43:44.700 --> 01:43:47.200
but I'm willing to step out and say,

01:43:47.200 --> 01:43:50.070
this is a pain in my existence and I wanna

01:43:50.070 --> 01:43:51.180
conquer this thing.

01:43:51.180 --> 01:43:53.593
Is anybody open to that?

01:43:53.593 --> 01:43:57.620
You have index cards, there
are more index cards available

01:43:57.620 --> 01:43:58.620
if you would like them.

01:43:58.620 --> 01:44:00.350
I would like to receive,

01:44:00.350 --> 01:44:04.225
and we would like to receive
your contact information.

01:44:04.225 --> 01:44:07.170
And what is that work that you're doing?

01:44:07.170 --> 01:44:09.570
What are those conversations
that you're having,

01:44:09.570 --> 01:44:12.480
or that you want to
start having around any

01:44:12.480 --> 01:44:14.550
of these topics or other topics?

01:44:14.550 --> 01:44:19.550
And my promise to you,
it's hard making a promise.

01:44:20.460 --> 01:44:24.470
My promise to you is to
collect that and send it back

01:44:24.470 --> 01:44:28.400
to every registrant who provided
an email address, to say,

01:44:28.400 --> 01:44:32.370
this is where we can continue
the conversation on campus.

01:44:32.370 --> 01:44:34.340
And this is the work that we can do.

01:44:34.340 --> 01:44:35.950
And I know that there are professionals

01:44:35.950 --> 01:44:40.950
in the audience who think
about these topics often.

01:44:41.360 --> 01:44:43.803
And I would like a way to,

01:44:45.240 --> 01:44:49.010
just to harvest your
generosity and to share

01:44:49.010 --> 01:44:54.010
it in ways that allow you
to keep your mental health

01:44:54.042 --> 01:44:56.010
in ways that support you.

01:44:56.010 --> 01:44:58.030
So if there's an index
card that you would like,

01:44:58.030 --> 01:45:00.450
please ask, if there's a
pen that you would like,

01:45:00.450 --> 01:45:04.450
please ask, and we'll
collect it before it's over.

01:45:04.450 --> 01:45:07.386
If you did not provide your email address,

01:45:07.386 --> 01:45:10.980
but you would like to
receive that information,

01:45:10.980 --> 01:45:12.960
please also write that on an index card

01:45:12.960 --> 01:45:14.350
and we will collect it.

01:45:14.350 --> 01:45:16.724
So I ask Lecrae to send
us off with something

01:45:16.724 --> 01:45:19.023
and then Sharon to come forward.

01:45:19.970 --> 01:45:24.083
- All I will say before I exit is like,

01:45:24.970 --> 01:45:27.500
I'm gonna leave,

01:45:27.500 --> 01:45:30.190
but you're gonna continue
to be here wrestling,

01:45:30.190 --> 01:45:34.290
and so I think it's very
crucial that you make

01:45:34.290 --> 01:45:38.083
these connections, and
with the leadership,

01:45:38.083 --> 01:45:40.410
the wisdom in this room and one another,

01:45:40.410 --> 01:45:42.020
and really foster this community,

01:45:42.020 --> 01:45:44.227
because it's very easy for
somebody like me to pop in,

01:45:44.227 --> 01:45:45.620
drop a bomb.

01:45:45.620 --> 01:45:47.097
Everyone's like yeah, that's good.

01:45:47.097 --> 01:45:49.483
And then I leave and that's it.

01:45:49.483 --> 01:45:50.563
But it's, hopefully I'm
here just as an inspiration

01:45:54.620 --> 01:45:56.890
for you to continue doing
what you're already doing

01:45:56.890 --> 01:45:59.750
and to continue to do more than that.

01:45:59.750 --> 01:46:01.829
So that's my hope and my prayer,

01:46:01.829 --> 01:46:06.829
that when I come back next
time it'll be even greater.

01:46:09.000 --> 01:46:10.140
So yeah.

01:46:10.140 --> 01:46:10.973
- Thank you.

01:46:11.940 --> 01:46:14.390
And those of you who are
willing to share your story

01:46:15.900 --> 01:46:18.480
and to be in conversation with someone,

01:46:18.480 --> 01:46:21.053
just to be that vulnerable, please say so.

01:46:30.801 --> 01:46:33.218
(indistinct)

01:46:38.550 --> 01:46:41.300
- I just wanna thank
everyone who came out.

01:46:41.300 --> 01:46:45.980
I wanna especially thank
OrLando and Nii who became new

01:46:45.980 --> 01:46:49.470
good old friends in these last few months,

01:46:49.470 --> 01:46:51.640
thank you for all the work that you did

01:46:51.640 --> 01:46:53.670
for all the volunteers.

01:46:53.670 --> 01:46:55.980
Thank you to Kim for coming.

01:46:55.980 --> 01:46:58.540
And Lecrae, thank you for
the gift of your words

01:46:58.540 --> 01:47:00.930
and your presence and your spirit.

01:47:00.930 --> 01:47:03.050
And as you were speaking,

01:47:03.050 --> 01:47:07.660
I was thinking, he's taking
words and he's reinvented

01:47:07.660 --> 01:47:10.830
them to the good, and in particular,

01:47:10.830 --> 01:47:15.830
I was thinking about failure
as birth, as a beginning,

01:47:17.750 --> 01:47:20.090
that when you talked about building

01:47:20.090 --> 01:47:23.640
on that and lifting your brokenness,

01:47:23.640 --> 01:47:27.460
it's something we all
need to hear at this place

01:47:27.460 --> 01:47:32.460
that demands perfection,
which I think can finish us,

01:47:32.570 --> 01:47:35.023
but failure can begin us.

01:47:35.023 --> 01:47:37.773
And so you gave that to us tonight.

01:47:38.750 --> 01:47:43.733
I wanna invite you all to
take a deep breath. (exhales)

01:47:46.300 --> 01:47:48.053
We're going back out there.

01:47:49.220 --> 01:47:51.870
But we're not going back out there alone.

01:47:51.870 --> 01:47:54.220
We shared some important things tonight.

01:47:54.220 --> 01:47:56.709
I thank you for your bravery.

01:47:56.709 --> 01:48:00.026
That's another word that we need to claim

01:48:00.026 --> 01:48:02.290
to a different end.

01:48:02.290 --> 01:48:05.110
A bravery that's radical
in how far and wide

01:48:05.110 --> 01:48:08.180
we embrace others and how far and wide

01:48:08.180 --> 01:48:12.770
we embrace ourselves and our brokenness.

01:48:12.770 --> 01:48:15.290
So God bless each and every one of you,

01:48:15.290 --> 01:48:18.200
go in peace and breathe.

01:48:18.200 --> 01:48:19.720
Good night.

01:48:19.720 --> 01:48:22.887
(audience applauding)

01:48:27.880 --> 01:48:30.463
(gentle music)

